From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: marty <marty@fyai.co.nz>
Date: Sun, 7 Sep 2026 16:45:00 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] RetroPlayer: do not destroy a context after deinitialising

Closing a hardware-rendered game segfaulted on the way out:

    pthread_mutex_lock
    CRenderBufferManager::GetBufferPools
    CRPRenderManager::DestroyContext
    CThread::Action

DestroyContext() is driven by the game loop's EndEvent(), which can arrive
after the player has destroyed CRPProcessInfo - and m_processInfo is a
reference to it, so the buffer manager's mutex was already freed.

Deinitialize() now releases the hardware buffer and pools itself, while process
info is still alive, and DestroyContext() returns early once that has happened.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/rendering/RPRenderManager.cpp	2026-09-07 17:03:30.882489536 +1200
+++ b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/rendering/RPRenderManager.cpp	2026-09-07 17:03:30.902937206 +1200
@@ -96,6 +96,12 @@
   }
   m_savestateBuffers.clear();
 
+  // The hardware side goes here too, not only in DestroyContext(). This runs
+  // while CRPProcessInfo is still alive; DestroyContext() is driven by the
+  // game loop's EndEvent() and can arrive after the player has torn it down.
+  ReleaseHwRenderBuffer();
+  m_hwBufferPools.clear();
+
   // Renderers may still hold GPU resources, so defer cleanup to the rendering
   // or destructor thread
   {
@@ -308,6 +314,19 @@
 
 void CRPRenderManager::DestroyContext()
 {
+  // Nothing to do once Deinitialize() has run, and m_processInfo below is a
+  // reference the player owns: closing a hardware-rendered game segfaulted on
+  // the way out, because the game loop's EndEvent() arrives after the player
+  // has destroyed it and this walked a freed mutex.
+  //
+  //   CRenderBufferManager::GetBufferPools
+  //   CRPRenderManager::DestroyContext
+  //
+  // Deinitialize() releases the hardware buffer and pools itself, while
+  // CRPProcessInfo is still alive, so there is nothing left to free here.
+  if (m_state == RENDER_STATE::UNCONFIGURED)
+    return;
+
   // Order matters: the buffer owns GL objects created in the pool's context, so
   // it has to go first. This runs from EndEvent(), on the game loop thread that
   // made the context current, which is the only thread allowed to free them.
